  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a single transit line in Hanoi, which may limit generalizability to cities with different institutional contexts or real estate dynamics.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the effects of construction completion and subsequent operational delays of Hanoi's first urban rail line on local land prices. Using a spatial difference-in-differences model combined with machine learning, the analysis found that completed construction initially boosted land prices by 3.76% in treatment zones, with the highest increases occurring 150 to 300 meters from construction sites. However, during the prolonged delay in starting operations, land prices within 750 meters of the stations fell by 3.45%.",
      "why": "The research measures the financial penalty of infrastructure delays, demonstrating that prolonged uncertainty can actively erode the land value premiums generated during the construction phase."
    }
  },
